rence in a sense you saw Adam  Schiff the lead house manager the  prosecutor  this case saying that there is no fair  trial without evidence and he's pointing  out this key point of contention that  really has incensed Democrats about this  Republican proposal and that is the lack  of a guarantee to introduce the  currently existing evidence into the  trial record what does that mean in  layman's terms well it means that there  will that the 17 witnesses who we've  heard from in the last month and the  text messages so much that we've  reported on that that would not warm any  sort of legal basis for the Senators to  consider when determining whether or not  the president is guilty of these charges  and thus should be removed from office  rather without that guarantee that this  evidence should be considered the  Senators may have an excuse to say that  there's no legal basis for the arguments  that we'll hear so we're really  Republicans trying to take away the  fundamental building blocks the legal  building blocks the Democrats say are  essential to arguing their case and to  proving what they think is abuse of  power and obstruction of Congress on  part of the president and that is what  has them so outraged because it is a  extreme departure from what we saw in  the last impeachment trial that of Bill  Clinton 21 years ago that is the house  evidence at that point or that the  Independent Counsel evidence was  automatically part of the trial record  for President Clinton's impeachment  trial that will not be the case here for  Trump it doesn't mean that it can't be  called into the record later on it just  means that postponing that decision and  and not having that guarantee means more  partisan opportunities for Republicans  to move to not include that evidence and  of course the same concerns regarding  the Democrats demand  four more witnesses to be called and  that is why you just heard the  Democratic prosecutors the house  managers say that this will not